@node install "Installation"
@toc main
@next flight

  Copy Astralbound.000 and Astralbound.020 to an Amiga drawer. Run from Shell:

  Astralbound.000 --ecs
  Astralbound.020 --aga
  Astralbound.020 --rtg

  Native modes require Kickstart 3.0 or later and detect PAL/NTSC.
  Add --lowres on PAL for a centred 320 x 200 display.
  AGA requires an AGA chipset. RTG needs CyberGraphX V41 or a
  Picasso96 setup with the compatible CyberGraphX API.
  Expansion RAM is needed; stock A500 performance is not established.

@endnode

@node flight "Flying Your Ship"
@toc main
@prev install
@next navigation

  L         Launch from a port or open ground.
  Arrows    Left/right roll; up/down pitch.
  RMouse    Hold and drag as a virtual stick; release to recenter.
  LMouse    Operate console; fire only in the flight viewport.
  Joystick  Port 2 directions steer; fire button shoots.
  W / S     Increase or decrease set speed.
  E         Toggle flight assist / direct thrust.
  A         Engage System Drive cruise / disengage and brake.
  Ctrl      Fire forward laser, or the aimed turret in turret views.
  K         Raise/lower landing gear while flying.
  P         Toggle autopilot to the selected port.
  T         Cycle port targets.
  Q         Cycle planet/moon approach waypoints.
  D         Request manual docking clearance.
  V         Return to flight; cycle front/rear/external/dorsal/ventral.
  R         Cycle contacts within the 10 km radar sphere.
  Z / I     Open orbital map / system and body data.
  Space     Pause/resume simulation.
  H         Show on-screen help.
  Escape    Ask whether to save before quitting; Escape again cancels.

  Mouse console: FLY returns to flight; VIEW cycles the camera. GAL opens
  3D galaxy map; NAV lists destinations; SYS shows clickable orbital markers.
  PORT or S calls the station services directory. LOG, KIT, CREW and HLP open
  the other panels. Select a row, then click its action button. Dim controls
  are unavailable. Release inside the pressed control to activate it; drag
  away to cancel. GUI clicks never fire the laser.

  The cockpit has launch, autopilot, drive, assist, gear, docking and contact
  buttons. Drag the cyan throttle strip; STOP commands zero speed. Click
  a time factor for 1 through 4096x; safety limits still apply. PAUSE/PLAY
  halts/resumes flight. Click the scanner for missile/decoy controls; click
  its background again to return. SAVE and LOAD are in the panels.
  Escape pauses the game and offers Save & Quit (S), Quit Without Saving (Q),
  or Cancel (Escape/C). A failed save keeps the dialog open.
  Overlapping orbital markers can be selected through NAV's destination
  list and BODY/PORT/PAGE buttons. Keyboard shortcuts remain available.

  Mouse: drag right/left for roll, up/down for pitch, with a small dead zone.
  Use the mouse port and a digital joystick in port 2. On the host preview,
  axes 0/1 or a direction pad steer, button 0 fires. W/S controls thrust.
  Vertical controls are inverted: down pitches up; up pitches down.
  This also applies to turret aiming. Pitch follows the ship's bank.
  Autopilot levels the bank and retains
  steering until you disengage it with P.

  In turret views arrows, mouse and joystick aim independently of the hull. W/S still controls
  ship thrust; Ctrl fires along turret aim. Pitch stops short of the poles.
  Radar follows hull heading, forward at top; stems show vertical distance.
  Red contacts are hostile, green friendly, gold selected. Selected contacts
  show a diamond, hull, range and signed closing speed. Navigation targets
  stay independent. Turret aim and contact selection reset on loading.

  Start with L, then P, to fly from Haven to its orbital Relay.
  Space preserves momentum. Turning does not instantly change velocity.
  With assist disabled, W/S applies forward/reverse thrust directly.
  Manual impacts can destroy the ship. Atmospheres cap air-relative speed
  at 1,200 m/s without overspeed damage. A manual descent into an
  atmosphere from space fires an emergency brake that stops above 10 km
  and leaves the ship hovering with assist on. Fast flight reads in c from 0.01c;
  1c is the speed of light. Bodies and stars in view are labelled in space;
  moons are labelled only when the ship is near their host planet.
  Lower gear and approach below 8 m/s relative to the port to dock.
  Orbital stations move and rotate. Match their velocity, request D
  clearance, then enter the central opening through the marked corridor.
  Autopilot requests clearance and waits outside the corridor if busy.

@endnode

@node navigation "Navigation and Time"
@toc main
@prev flight
@next contracts

  [ / ] decrease/increase time: 1, 4, 16, 64, 256, 1024, 4096x.
  High rates require autopilot and clear space; hazards reduce the rate.

  Y opens the system destination list. T selects inhabited ports;
  Q selects body waypoints. Up/Down changes the selected list's target.
  Z opens live top-down orbits. C toggles system/local scale around the
  selected body, or its parent for moons. Q/T and Up/Down select destinations.
  Local scale resolves moons and stations. White stars, green settled bodies,
  cyan other bodies, gold target and red ship use true-scale projected orbits.
  Small bodies and ports can overlap at system scale.
  I, or a second click on the selected body, opens its data sheet: radius,
  density, mass, gravity, escape speed, mean temperature, surface pressure,
  atmospheric composition, day and orbital period, distance, ports, survey
  status and navigation range. Solar System values are measured; other
  systems show values consistent with each world's climate.
  Y returns to the list. V returns to flight, then P engages its autopilot.
  Body waypoints stop above the atmosphere. Gas giants cannot be landed on.
  Autopilot follows elevated waypoints around planets and central stars.

  Planets have realistic radii and astronomical orbital separations.
  Stars, planets, moons and stations move in analytic circular orbits.
  Flight velocities are relative to the current translated local frame.
  Every hull has a System Drive for rapid in-system acceleration/braking.
  A toggles the drive; P toggles autopilot. Neither changes the other.
  In manual flight A selects full cruise; W/S adjusts speed; A again brakes.
  With autopilot engaged, guidance controls speed with the selected drive.
  Maximum acceleration is 20,000,000 m/s/s; cruise cap is 30c.
  Hyperspace arrives 1.1 million km above the host star's surface, just
  outside its drive exclusion shell.
  Bodies, atmosphere, stars, stations and traffic lower the speed limit.
  Inside an exclusion zone or with no fuel the drive cannot run: the
  DRIVE button is greyed and A is refused. An armed drive that can no
  longer run disengages, zeroing set speed and the engine note.
  DRIVE / AUTO DRIVE identifies operation; DRIVE LOCK means autopilot
  or laser fire is briefly withholding it. Local flight uses ordinary engines.
  Autopilot uses the drive only when you arm it with A; otherwise it uses
  ordinary engines. Guidance limits speed for detours, approach and docking.
  Body arrival zeros set speed.
  Fuel scales with engine output fraction, including drive operation.
  Loading resets drive controls but preserves velocity and cruise braking.

  ] raises requested time compression; [ reduces it.
  Compression works in manual flight, with autopilot, in combat,
  near terrain and traffic, and while landed or docked.
  Missiles and flight-mode changes preserve the selected rate.
  Arrival, docking and loss of autopilot thrust authority return to 1x.
  The HUD shows one actual multiplier, for example TIME 256X.
  Pause still stops time.
  Remote ordinary cruise uses bounded intervals up to 128 seconds.
  System Drive cruise intervals are capped at one simulated second.
  Travel, moving bodies and braking room are checked before each interval.
  Departure, approach, docking and combat use 1/32-second physics ticks.
  Work is limited to 128 simulation steps per displayed frame. The HUD
  shows the delivered rate, which may be below the requested compression.

  G opens the 3D galaxy map. Drag to rotate; +/- zoom. PAN changes
  dragging to translation. Arrows rotate; WASD pans; Q/E moves vertically.
  HOME/C centres on the current system, FOCUS/F on the selected target.
  GALAXY opens the sampled spiral-disc overview. Zoom to 8 ly or closer
  for all systems in the local 17-sector cube. Click any star to target it.
  SEARCH or / starts name entry. Type a name or alias, Up/Down selects
  a result, Return targets it. Click result rows or PREV/NEXT to browse.
  Backspace edits, Escape leaves search, CLEAR clears the query.
  Search covers all 94 real entries, local sampled stars and visited systems.
  INFO shows stellar components, distance, bodies, ports and local records.
  WAYPOINT pins a cyan diamond/line independently of the gold target box;
  click WAYPOINT again on the same target to remove it. The current system
  has a red cross; green dots mark visited systems. Hover shows a name.
  X/JUMP travels above 12 km with sufficient fuel, no nearby traffic,
  and a destination within six light-years. Distances use full 3D positions.
  The hyperspace drive charges for 3 seconds, then the ship crosses
  a hyperspace tunnel to the destination. Traffic arriving during the
  charge aborts the jump at no cost. Only pause works meanwhile.
  J shows contract destination sectors and port numbers.

  Real stars include Sol, Proxima/Alpha Centauri, Sirius, Tau Ceti, Vega,
  Polaris, Rigel, Betelgeuse and Deneb. Systems support up to six components.
  Normal stars, red giants, brown/white dwarfs, neutron stars and black
  holes have distinct types. HYG J2000 positions retain 0.001 ly precision;
  companion orbits and flight radii are game approximations. Catalog data
  is CC BY-SA 4.0 (David Nash / Astronomy Nexus); see data/README.md.
  The galaxy has exactly 1,000,000,000 systems, including the 94 real
  entries and nine starter systems. Generated systems occupy a finite
  fictional disc 20,000 ly across and 600 ly thick. Space beyond is empty.
  Sol has eight planets, five dwarf planets and 21 major moons:
  34 navigable bodies plus the Sun. Minor bodies and small irregular or
  provisional moons are omitted. Planets use eccentric Kepler orbits,
  real radii and periods. Earth/Mars/Moon ports are fictional.
  Old saves remap retained bodies. Removed bodies move you to Earth orbit;
  removed systems return to Hetha's starting port. Removed rigs refund
  500 CR and contracts to removed systems cancel without reputation loss.
  Unsettled systems have no ports; use Q and P to explore their bodies.
  Green map markers show previously visited systems. The commander
  journal records up to 128 systems and their visited/surveyed bodies.
  An approach below 100 km marks a body surveyed.

@endnode

@node contracts "Port Contracts"
@toc main
@prev navigation
@next trade

  Dock, press S or click PORT to call station services. Select a named
  contact to see their portrait, then Call/Return to open the service.
  The directory offers contracts, trade, hulls, equipment, repairs and crew.
  Authorise 100 CR at engineering restores hull, shields and fuel.

  B opens contract notices directly. Click an offer or press 1-8 to open
  its briefing. Return opens a selected notice; Return in its briefing
  accepts it. Story gives the client's background, Orders gives the exact
  flight objective, and Terms shows requirements and acceptance blockers.
  J opens your selectable log, including completed and failed contracts.
  Acceptance opens the live contract briefing. Deadlines use simulated time.

  Destination opens the galaxy map focused on the mission system. Waypoint
  marks it for navigation. Existing waypoints stay until you choose a new
  one. Briefing returns to the same contract and tab. For a local mission,
  Destination also selects its port; NAV/SYS shows local navigation.
  The briefing names the destination port and current delivery leg.

  Courier / Freight: dock at the destination sector and port.
  Freight reserves cargo space until the contract ends.

  Orbit Patrol / Bounty: a hostile appears above 13 km altitude.
  Air Patrol: a hostile appears between 600 and 6000 m.
  Disable the target with the laser to complete the combat contract.

  Survey: fly between 500 and 6000 m below 200 m/s for twenty seconds.

  Payment is automatic and occurs once. Expired contracts fail.
  B again switches board pages. + negotiates once at reputation 3:
  20% more pay with 25% less remaining time. The board shows reputation,
  cargo and passenger requirements. Jobs cannot be accepted while wanted.

  Passenger: reserve cabin seats and deliver to the named port.
  Rescue: collect the distress pod above 13 km with N, then deliver.
  Escort: stay within 5 km for 120 seconds and keep the civilian alive.
  Recon: 30 seconds within 10 km of the surface port, at 500-6000 m
  and below 200 m/s relative to rotating ground.
  Installation: destroy the separate ground target near the surface port.
  Assassination: destroy the target; a rival faction issues a 1000 CR fine.
  Multi-leg: visit three ports in the destination system. J shows each leg.
  These are bounded templates. Payment occurs only after the final objective.

@endnode

@node trade "Trading and Ship Services"
@toc main
@prev contracts
@next careers

  M opens the market. Select a commodity with 1-8.
  + buys one tonne; - sells one tonne. Transactions require docking.
  Your hold includes space reserved by active freight contracts.
  The screen shows separate buy and sell prices for each commodity.
  Each port keeps its own supply. Purchases raise scarcity prices;
  selling increases stock and lowers prices. Supply recovers towards
  its generated level by one tonne per simulated hour, even while away.
  Saves preserve up to 128 traded ports across jumps. A full ledger
  refuses trades at new ports; existing markets remain usable.

  U opens the shipyard.
  F refuels and repairs for 100 credits.
  O buys a laser upgrade for 2000 credits, up to three upgrades.
  1-6 or Up/Down selects a hull, Return buys it; N buys the next catalogue hull.
  The selected hull turns in a 3D preview beside bars that compare its
  hold, crew, acceleration, mass, missiles and turrets with your own
  (white tick). The status line explains any refusal before you buy.
  L launches again.

  Cinder/Loom/Hearth: 20/60/140 tonnes. Kestrel/Quarry/Wayfarer:
  35/90/180 tonnes. Larger hulls need crew; see Careers.
  The full equipment catalogue and civilian economy are unfinished.

@endnode

@node careers "Careers, Mining and Equipment"
@toc main
@prev trade
@next saving

  9 opens crew/factions. + recruits for 200 CR; - dismisses.
  Crew cost 25 CR each per simulated hour, including jumps and warp.
  Unpaid crew leave; launch requires the hull's minimum crew.
  Aurora Union, Meridian League and Free Reach keep independent records.
  Completed jobs raise reputation; failed jobs lower it. Military jobs
  advance service/rank, with a medal every five jobs. F here pays fines.

  U opens six hulls: 1-6 and Return buys. Mass, acceleration, hold, crew,
  shapes, turret mounts and missile capacities differ. Cargo adds mass.
  Purchases refuse incompatible cargo, crew, missiles or turret mounts.
  0 opens equipment: 1-8 and Return buys; - sells at half price.
  Cabins have two seats and take two tonnes each. O upgrades the selected
  dorsal/ventral turret, or the forward laser otherwise (2000 CR, 3 levels).
  Starting Cinder turrets are fitted. Sell unsupported mounts before
  buying another hull. R selects radar contacts; comma launches a missile.
  Period releases a decoy, suppressing nearby hostile fire for 12 seconds.
  Slash uses a pod before hull reaches zero: rescue takes an hour and loses
  hull, cargo, crew, equipment and contracts, then loans a Cinder at a port.
  Unsettled systems have no pod-rescue destination.

  Governments 2/3 restrict crystals; 3 also restricts circuits.
  B in M market toggles black-market access at lawless ports or reputation
  5+. Restricted goods cost 50% more. Customs confiscates contraband on
  departure, arrival and patrol scans: 100 CR fine per tonne.
  Two police ships patrol every port and station. Firing within 10 km
  of police costs a 500 CR fine; firing again, or shooting at police,
  makes every patrol hostile until the fine is paid with F at a port.
  Returning fire on an attacker is lawful. Fines of 1000 CR or more are
  a warrant: police engage near ports. Police ships are armoured, repair
  themselves and kill within seconds - pay up or run.
  Lawless space has pirates.

  Backslash selects an asteroid above the first body; P approaches 400 m
  behind it. Aim and O with mining equipment releases ore. Within 250 m
  and below 50 m/s relative speed, N collects ore, salvage or distress pods.
  Each asteroid has four tonnes; depletion survives saves/jumps.
  New mining systems are refused when the 32-system deposit ledger fills.
  Rocks have collision geometry: do not fly into them.

  Land on open ground and O deploys a 500 CR rig. N recovers within 500 m
  of that site, loading available hold space. Rigs produce one tonne/hour
  of alloys, up to 20 tonnes. An emptied rig sells for 250 CR.
  Up to four rigs persist across jumps/saves. Semicolon selects a rig
  waypoint in the current system; P stops 100 m above it for manual landing.
  Pods are local to a system.
  A fuel scoop gathers one fuel point/second in the upper two thirds of
  hydrogen atmosphere at 50-800 m/s relative to its air.

@endnode

@node saving "Commander Saves"
@toc main
@prev careers
@next hardware

  F5 saves to PROGDIR:astralbound.sav. F9 loads the commander.
  Native modes restore the OS for file access, then resume takeover.
  The save includes its format/generator version and checksum, exploration
  journal, local frame, accumulated fuel impulse, docking approach and markets.
  Save format 9 stores the galaxy waypoint, Sol survey masks and ship roll,
  and migrates the reduced population/body catalog from earlier saves.
  Generated planetary streams retain version 3. Version 3 commanders load
  with saved supply assigned to the docked port; other ports use generated
  supply. Old version 2 prototype commanders remain incompatible.
  A rejected or corrupt load leaves the current game unchanged.

  New data is written and closed before replacing the old slot.
  On filesystems that cannot replace via Rename, the old slot is kept
  as astralbound.sav.bak. A failed replacement attempts to restore it.
  Versions 3 through 7 load with empty new Sol records; 3 through 6
  load with zero bank. Version 4 and 5 commanders load, with default faction records
  and the crew needed by their hull. Careers/equipment/resources are saved. Old open-ground landings are attached to
  the rotating terrain; docked commanders follow their moving port.
  Prototype save formats can change between development revisions.

@endnode

@node hardware "Hardware Backends"
@toc main
@prev saving
@next limits

  ECS uses PAL 320 x 256 or NTSC 320 x 200, five planes, 32 colours.
  --lowres selects centred 320 x 200 on PAL, reducing pixel work.
  At 200 lines, menus use the whole screen to keep all rows visible.
  Native timing uses 50 Hz PAL or 60 Hz NTSC with fixed physics ticks.
  AGA uses eight planes, 256 palette entries with 24-bit precision,
  64-bit display fetches and extra terrain/atmosphere colour ranges.
  Native output owns its Copper lists and double-buffered Chip RAM.
  OS scheduling and interrupts are disabled during native play.

  RTG uses a 640 x 480 driver-managed display and safe buffer messages.
  Its OS remains running because the graphics-card driver requires it.
  This is the portable RTG path; direct registers differ between cards.

  Simulation uses integer coordinates, a lookup trig table and fixed
  physics ticks. Astralbound.000/.020/.030 require no FPU. The FPU builds give
  identical simulation results and only speed up rendering maths.

@endnode

@node limits "Current Prototype Limits"
@toc main
@prev hardware
@next help

  Orbits are inclined circles, not a full gravitational N-body solver.
  Gravity follows the nearest body and adds solar gravity in space.
  Terrain LOD edges share vertices and use a skirt to the coarse globe.
  Surfaces have seeded rock strata, mineral deposits, desert dune ridges,
  ice fractures, vegetation clearings and lunar crater/ejecta patterns.
  Detail stays anchored to the rotating planet and fades with distance.
  Physical terrain also varies by body type: rocky mountain ranges, hills,
  valleys and volcanic cones/calderas; desert plateaus and winding canyons;
  glacial ridges and rifts; lunar crater bowls with raised rims. Some rocky
  moons have volcanoes. Ocean worlds have level seas and inland ranges.
  Gas giants have no solid relief. Volcanoes are static, without eruptions.
  Terrain is bounded to 8 km elevation and shares geometry with collision.
  Detail extends to 20 km above sea level; distant slopes retain lighting.
  Landing pads blend smoothly into the surrounding terrain. Older saves
  below regenerated ground are lifted clear when loaded.

  Planets rotate about their Z axes. Terrain, forests and surface ports
  follow that rotation; port velocities include ground motion. Moons are
  landable and gas giants can have multiple satellites and equatorial rings.
  Stellar geometry controls day/night and planet/moon eclipses. Atmosphere
  colours approximate Rayleigh single scattering above the solar horizon.
  Temperate/frozen weather has shaded cumulus clouds that tint the view
  when flown through, rain and snow. Flora grows on temperate land, with polar ice and dry rock.
  Clouds and precipitation are bounded procedural visual models, without
  fluid dynamics, accumulation or hydrology. Trees/buildings are decorative.
  The renderer uses a depth buffer. Docking walls have swept collisions.
  Clearance traffic is scheduled; it is not a persistent civilian fleet.

  Careers use thirteen templates, three factions and six hulls.
  Mining records 32 systems with eight four-tonne asteroids each, plus
  four persistent rigs. Eight cargo pods and eight missiles are local.
  Local NPCs are mission/police/pirate encounters, not persistent fleets.
  Strategic wars and a fully simulated civilian economy remain outside
  this model. Physical hardware and long-duration play still need QA.
  Analogue/CD32 input and RTG high-colour are implemented; physical QA remains.
  NTSC ECS output has emulator evidence; physical PAL/NTSC QA remains.
  Real-hardware frame rates have not been established.

  Passing specific regression tests does not prove absence of all bugs.
  See the source repository's docs/roadmap.md for the full development list.

@endnode

@node help "Quick Help"
@toc main
@prev limits

  Launch L / Port T / Body Q / Auto P / Clearance D / Flight V
  System ports Y / Galaxy G / Jump X / Contract log J
  Services S / Contracts B / Market M / Shipyard U / Equipment 0 / Crew 9
